docs/research/2026-10-29-analyst-standard/reading-week.md:571:- luna-1 unmatched (17): e2: The Singapore Police Force said it issued a prohibition of disposal order against a residence valued at around S$55 million ($42.4 million) and seized around S$1 million in funds in connection with fraud investigations. ‖ e3: Aaron Woon and Alan Wei were charged with criminal conspiracy to commit fraud, and Li Ming was charged with fraud by false representation, in February 2025. ‖ e4: Jenny Lim was charged in connection with the case in April 2026. ‖ e5: Investigators allege that Aperia International, A-Speed Infotech, and Aperia Cloud Services concealed the actual end-user of servers; Woon, Wei, and Lim were key officers of the companies at the time of the alleged offences. ‖ e6: Investigators found Li to be the controller of Luxuriate Your Life, which was allegedly involved in the scheme. ‖ e8: Police announced additional fraud and money-laundering charges on July 1, 2026: seven each for Lim and Woon, and seven further charges for Wei scheduled for July 6. ‖ e9: Fraud charges allege Woon, Wei, and Lim conspired to commit fraud in purchasing servers from Dell, Super Micro Computer, and Asus. ‖ e10: The charges allege that Woon, Wei, and Lim falsely represented to suppliers from November 2023 through February 2025 that an Aperia Group company would be the servers' end-user. ‖ e11: Four companies are due to face eight fraud-by-false-representation charges on July 6, 2026; the SPF described this as the first prosecution of corporate entities in these investigations. ‖ e12: Police said Lim and Woon were charged with money laundering after each acquired approximately S$1.2 million in personal bank accounts, which police said pointed to benefits from criminal conduct. ‖ e13: Wei faces three money-laundering counts relating to the seized mansion and an additional S$5.8 deposited in his personal account; police allege the deposit was partly proceeds of criminal conduct. ‖ e14: Li faced additional fraud charges alleging misrepresentation of the end-user of servers Luxuriate Your Life intended to buy from Super Micro. ‖ e15: In February 2025, media reported that the U.S. Commerce Department was investigating whether DeepSeek had obtained restricted Nvidia chips through third parties in countries including Malaysia and Singapore. ‖ e16: Singapore said it was unaware of smuggling in the country and would act firmly against individuals or entities found to breach the law. ‖ e17: The Singapore Police Force said it takes a zero-tolerance stance toward such offenses and will act against businesses or individuals who violate Singaporean law. ‖ e18: Malaysian authorities said they foiled an attempt to smuggle advanced AI chips through the country's main airport the previous week, seizing 72 servers worth nearly $13 million. ‖ e19: Singapore police reported that the number of such cases rose from 589 in the first half of 2024 to 1,762 in the first half of 2026.
docs/research/2026-10-29-analyst-standard/reading-week.md:572:- luna-2 unmatched (19): e2: The Singapore Police Force said on July 1, 2026, that it issued a prohibition of disposal order against a residence valued at around S$55 million ($42.4 million) and seized around S$1 million in funds. ‖ e3: Aaron Woon and Alan Wei were charged with criminal conspiracy to commit fraud, and Li Ming was charged with fraud by false representation, in February 2025. ‖ e4: Jenny Lim was charged in connection with the case in April 2026. ‖ e5: Authorities allege that Aperia International, A-Speed Infotech, and Aperia Cloud Services concealed the actual end-user of servers during the alleged offences. ‖ e6: Investigators found Li Ming to be the controller of Luxuriate Your Life, which was allegedly involved in the scheme. ‖ e8: Singapore police announced additional fraud and money-laundering charges against the defendants on July 1, 2026. ‖ e9: The Singapore Police Force said Lim and Woon each received seven new charges on July 1, 2026, and Wei was set to receive seven new charges on July 6, 2026. ‖ e10: Fraud charges allege a conspiracy involving purchases of servers from Dell, Super Micro Computer, and Asus. ‖ e11: The charges allege that from November 2023 through February 2025, the trio falsely represented to the suppliers that an Aperia Group company would be the end-user of the servers. ‖ e12: The Singapore Police Force said four companies were due to face a total of eight fraud-by-false-representation charges on July 6, 2026, the first corporate prosecutions in these investigations. ‖ e13: Police said Lim and Woon were each charged with money laundering after acquiring approximately S$1.2 million in their personal bank accounts. ‖ e14: Wei faces three money-laundering counts related to the seized mansion and an additional S$5.8 deposited in his personal account; the article does not specify a unit after S$5.8. ‖ e15: Li faced additional fraud charges alleging misrepresentation of the end-user of servers Luxuriate Your Life intended to purchase from Super Micro. ‖ e16: A report in February 2025 said the U.S. Commerce Department was investigating whether DeepSeek had obtained restricted Nvidia chips, potentially sourced through third parties in Malaysia and Singapore. ‖ e17: Singapore said it was unaware of smuggling in the country and would act firmly against individuals or entities found to break the law. ‖ e18: The Singapore Police Force said it would act resolutely against businesses or individuals who violate Singapore law. ‖ e19: Malaysia promised to close loopholes that could enable advanced semiconductors to move through its jurisdiction. ‖ e20: Malaysian authorities said they foiled an attempted smuggling operation at the country’s main airport and seized 72 servers worth nearly $13 million in the week before July 2, 2026. ‖ e21: Singaporean police reported that the number of such cases rose from 589 in the first half of 2024 to 1,762 in the first half of 2026.
docs/research/2026-10-29-analyst-standard/reading-week.md:573:- luna-3 unmatched (21): e2: The Singapore Police Force said on 2026-07-01 that it issued a prohibition of disposal order against a residence valued at around S$55 million ($42.4 million) and seized around S$1 million in funds in connection with fraud investigations. ‖ e3: Aaron Woon and Alan Wei were charged with criminal conspiracy to commit fraud, and Li Ming with fraud by false representation, in February 2025. ‖ e4: Jenny Lim was charged in connection with the case in April 2026. ‖ e5: The article reports that Woon, Wei, and Lim were key officers of Aperia International, A-Speed Infotech, and Aperia Cloud Services, alleged to have concealed the servers’ actual end-user. ‖ e6: Investigators found Li to be the controller of Luxuriate Your Life, which was allegedly involved in the scheme. ‖ e8: Some media reports suggested the servers shipped to Malaysia may have been destined for China, though their ultimate destination was unclear. ‖ e9: Singapore police announced additional fraud and money-laundering charges against the defendants on 2026-07-01. ‖ e10: The SPF said Lim and Woon each received seven new charges on 2026-07-01, and Wei was set to receive seven new charges on 2026-07-06. ‖ e11: Fraud charges allege Woon, Wei, and Lim conspired to commit fraud in connection with server purchases from Dell, Super Micro Computer, and Asus. ‖ e12: The charges allege that between November 2023 and February 2025, Woon, Wei, and Lim falsely represented to suppliers that an Aperia Group company would be the servers’ end-user. ‖ e13: The SPF said four companies were due to face a total of eight fraud-by-false-representation charges on 2026-07-06, the first corporate prosecutions in these investigations. ‖ e14: Police said Lim and Woon were charged with money laundering after each acquired approximately S$1.2 million in personal bank accounts, which police said pointed to benefits from criminal conduct. ‖ e15: Wei faced three money-laundering counts relating to the seized mansion and an additional S$5.8 deposited in his personal account, which police alleged partly represented criminal proceeds. ‖ e16: Li faced additional fraud charges concerning an alleged misrepresentation of the end-user of servers Luxuriate Your Life intended to buy from Super Micro. ‖ e17: Media reports in February 2025 said the U.S. Commerce Department was investigating whether DeepSeek had obtained restricted Nvidia chips through third parties in countries including Malaysia and Singapore. ‖ e18: Singapore said it was unaware of smuggling in the country and would act firmly against individuals or entities found to break the law. ‖ e19: The SPF said it had a zero-tolerance stance toward these offences and would act resolutely against businesses or individuals violating Singapore law. ‖ e20: Malaysian authorities said they foiled an attempt to smuggle advanced AI chips through the country’s main airport and seized 72 servers worth nearly $13 million during the week before 2026-07-02. ‖ e21: The article reports that the U.S. government is probing whether the Hangzhou-based AI firm built its R1 model using restricted Nvidia chips. ‖ e22: The U.S. government accused the conglomerate and its chairman, Chen Zhi, of running a network of forced-labor scam centers. ‖ e23: Singapore police said the number of such cases increased from 589 in the first half of 2024 to 1,762 in the first half of 2026.
docs/research/2026-10-29-analyst-standard/reading-week.md:574:- haiku-1 unmatched (11): e1: Singapore Police Force issued a prohibition of disposal order on a residence valued at about S$55 million ($42.4 million) and seized about S$1 million of funds in connection with fraud investigations. ‖ e2: Aaron Woon and Alan Wei were charged in February 2025 with criminal conspiracy to commit fraud, Li Ming was charged at the same time with fraud by false representation, and Jenny Lim was charged in April 2026. ‖ e4: Police charge Aperia Group defendants with conspiring to defraud Dell, Super Micro and Asus over server purchases, with false end-user representations made between November 2023 and February 2025. ‖ e5: Four companies in the case are due to face a total of eight fraud-by-false-representation charges to be announced on July 6, 2026, the first corporate prosecutions in these investigations. ‖ e6: Lim and Woon were each handed seven new charges, and Wei is set to receive seven new charges on July 6, 2026. ‖ e7: Police say Lim and Woon were each charged with money laundering after each acquired about SG$1.2 million in personal bank accounts, pointing to benefits from criminal conduct. ‖ e8: Wei faces three money laundering counts tied to the seized mansion and an additional S$5.8 (as printed) deposited in his personal account. ‖ e9: Li Ming faces additional fraud charges for misrepresenting the end-user of servers that Luxuriate Your Life intended to buy from Super Micro. ‖ e10: Malaysian authorities foiled an attempt to smuggle advanced AI chips through the main airport, seizing 72 servers worth nearly $13 million, in the week before the item. ‖ e11: Singaporean police say the number of such cases rose from 589 in H1 2024 to 1,762 in H1 2026, more than tripling. ‖ e12: Reports said in February 2025 that the U.S. Commerce Department was investigating whether DeepSeek accessed restricted Nvidia chips, possibly sourced via Malaysia and Singapore.
