     1	# The slice (D450): one fresh day through the wired path, live — reading (2026-11-13)
     2	
     3	**Answer: the wiring works live.** Both draws completed (`poc/data/clean-e2e-slice-2026-11-13/`: clean `s1`, production `sp1`, both
     4	2026-07-01, launched 05:31:38, ended 05:39:40); every component ran with real sends on one shared ledger; no stop, no overrun, no refusal,
     5	no retry; 60 ledger lines, every one attributed to its component, summing to the ledger's spent exactly. A wiring check, not a draw and not
     6	evidence of quality (WIRING-DESIGN.md §G).
     7	
     8	## Spend and time
     9	
    10	| component | time | attempts | ledger charge | archive tokens at `model.PRICES` | note |
    11	|---|---|---|---|---|---|
    12	| Step 1 (c8, 13 items, sequential) | 7 min 23 s | 13 | $0.1324 | $0.1324 | equal |
    13	| Step 2 (embeddings 1, Jev 22) | 3 s | 23 | $0.0110 | Jev $0.0036 | Jev charged at three times its tokens, embeddings likewise (5,874 tokens) |
    14	| cases | 1 s | 0 | 0 | — | 2 cases, equal to the theses the trace opened |
    15	| ladder step (pass 1) | 16 s | 2 | $0.0021 | $0.0024 at the full input rate | the ledger counts cached input at its share |
    16	| judge | 18 s | 2 | $0.0019 | $0.0021 at the full input rate | same |
    17	| production v118 (16 workers) | 41 s | 20 (LLM 15, Jev 5) | $0.0119 | $0.0150 at the full rate, Jev once | same |
    18	| **total** | | 60 | **$0.1593** | about $0.152 at `model.call`'s formula | |
    19	
    20	Spend under D439: $7.11 before, about $0.15 now (ledger $0.16): **about $7.26 of $40**.
    21	
    22	**Projection for the full run** (an estimate from this day's per-item rates and the stored runs, labelled as such): Step 1 about $0.010
    23	an item → about $2.5 a clean draw (243 items), sequential about 2 h 20 min a draw; Step 2 about $0.36 of ledger a draw (the stored u1);
    24	the ladder step three passes about $0.04 a draw; the judge about $0.14 a draw (Stage B); production about $0.45 a draw (q1–q3). Three of
    25	each: about **$10.4 of ledger**, inside $15.00 − $0.16.
    26	
    27	## What each component produced (read by Claude, every answer)
    28	
    29	- **Step 1** (13 answers beside their articles): two theses opened, the same two matters the stored draw u1 opened that day: the amount of
    30	  credit support NVIDIA will have extended to AI cloud partners at its next annual report (from NVIDIA's own announcement), and whether
    31	  NVIDIA will guarantee OpenAI's Ohio data-centre lease by 2027-12-31 (from The Information's report carried by moomoo). Seven evidence
    32	  items link to them (the announcement, Sharon AI and Firmus as first partners, the CoreWeave $6.3bn backstop, the later $3.5bn lease
    33	  guarantees, the equity stakes, the CFO's blog post). The other articles (Intel's $5bn stake and Xeon 6 choice, Valar's nuclear data
    34	  centre, Wistron in Texas, NN's liquid cooling, Palantir and Nemotron, Verkada, an RTX 5090 price piece, an agentic-RL blog post, Burry's
    35	  bets) opened nothing and their statements are unlinked, as the standard's "lean cautious" asks. One field to log: the Ohio item's
    36	  original party is "NVIDIA" for The Information's report about NVIDIA (the known original-speaker class), not a wiring matter.
    37	- **Step 2:** 23 records, 20 new groups, 3 repeats, 0 refused, 0 pending, 0 unresolved links.
    38	- **The ladder step:** the credit-support thesis → a figure ladder (USD, five bands from below $1bn to $30bn and above, the lower end
    39	  favourable, due 2027-02-28 at the FY2027 report's expected publication); the Ohio thesis → an event ladder (signed by 2027-12-31 against
    40	  formally abandoned; the amount marked not judged, one bet per question). Both pass the step's checks at the first request; the
    41	  configuration is the frozen one (`clean-ladder-stage-a d3b4a5f696c7cc82`).
    42	- **The judge:** credit support at rung 3 ($5bn–$15bn), implied about $10bn ($6.3bn CoreWeave + $3.5bn lease guarantees ≈ $9.8bn, the new
    43	  model unquantified), every one of the 8 evidence items it cited shown in its request; the Ohio guarantee at rung 3 (unresolved at the due
    44	  day), reading the $500bn as the asset's cost, not the guarantee. Twelve quotes cut by the judge at 400 characters (its frozen
    45	  behaviour).
    46	- **Production:** 13 items, 0 failed; one view (the credit-support amount, due 2027-02-28), judged rung 2 ($1bn–$5bn), anchored on the
    47	  $3.5bn lease guarantees; its five evidence items do not include the CoreWeave backstop. It did not open the Ohio guarantee. One day,
    48	  one draw: an observation of where the two systems differ, not a comparison.
    49	
    50	## What the slice does not show
    51	
    52	The ladder step's passes 2 and 3; a draw that opens many theses; the judge over many days (the due-day guard, changes, dependencies);
    53	Stage A's and Stage B's readings; any quality line. Those are the full run's (WIRING-DESIGN.md §E).
     1	# The product condition, end to end over two weeks with the library kept: the reading (D563), 2026-11-16
     2	
     3	**Answer: in this one draw, with the library of week 1 held, the six questions week 2 opened duplicated none it held (both
     4	families), and the step that writes answer choices wrote them for 12 of 13 topics in all three attempts, but the choices do not meet Alex's six lines (gate 1: 7 or 5 of 13
     5	topics right against a scaled 11). Of the 12 last answers, none is unsupported by both families and 6 are contested
     6	by at least one of three readers, for several different reasons. The company's own numbers have no home by product rule, and
     7	neither reader family predicted that any not delivered would change one of the 12 answers; two statements over-read their
     8	passages; the product's page could not show the new system's library (fixed at the writer, D564). One draw, one company,
     9	one fortnight; week 2's later steps were regenerated with week 1's, not a live two-week history.**
    10	
    11	Run: `poc/data/clean-carried-run-2026-11-16/` (week 1 the stored run of 2026-09-22..28 from empty; week 2 2026-09-29..10-05, 241
    12	never-sent articles, read from week 1's library; the later steps regenerated over the fortnight; ledger $3.476 of $6.00, no
    13	refusal). Plan frozen before any answer was read (`PLAN.md`, sha256 in `PLAN.sha256`); my own read stored first (`OWN-READ.md`).
    14	
    15	## What the run did
    16	
    17	| | Week 1 (from empty) | Week 2 (library kept) |
    18	|---|---|---|
    19	| Articles | 235 | 241 |
    20	| Topics opened | 7 | 6 |
    21	| Kept statements with no link | 389 of 569 (68%) | 339 of 626 (54%) |
    22	
    23	Answer choices over the fortnight: **12 of 13 topics written in all three attempts**; the one declined in all three is
    24	"venture funding for physical AI companies" (no listed theme covers it; the two opening readers split on its worth, my own read says not worth holding). Written is not right: read against
    25	Alex's six lines (reading A below), they do not meet them. For comparison, from an empty start: the week of 10-06, 4 of 13
    26	topics declined at least once, 2 every time, and agreed faults beyond refusals; July, 21 of 104 declined in two or three attempts.
    27	Different weeks and articles: an observation, not a measured difference.
    28	
    49	**Gate 2:**
    50	- Not met. Its control line has 4 of 8 comparable controls. D592: a new reference is Alex's call, and an unproven two-step route also exists.
    51	- It was measured on luna's questions and must be re-measured on astra's.
    52	
    53	**Gates 3 to 5:** owed, proposed, and partly built.
    54	
    55	**Cost:**
    56	- One company and 14 days, with astra substituted at a trial's average.
    57	- The clean system with astra is about $0.25 to $0.27 a day, against the running system's $0.04 to $0.05, about 5 to 7 times.
    58	- The astra step itself is about $0.04 a day.
    59	- This does not forecast 1,000 assets.
